export const textbook = {"title": "Drone Engineering from Zero", "sections": [{"id": "s0", "title": "Chapter 1: Your Drone, Your Tools, and the Language of Measurement", "html": "<p>By the end of this chapter you can name every major part of your drone and say what it does, convert units correctly in multi-step drone problems, and write Python that uses functions, lists, dictionaries, and classes.</p>\n<p>The chapter has three parts. Part A (sections 1.1 to 1.4) is the drone itself, so you know what you are building before the math begins. Part B (1.5 and 1.6) is measurement, the skill that keeps every later calculation honest. Part C (1.7) is a Python refresher built around code like the robot code you already wrote.</p>\n", "items": [], "kind": "cp"}, {"id": "s1", "title": "What you will be able to do", "html": "<ul>\n<li><p>Explain how four motors turn into forward flight, hover, and rotation.</p></li>\n<li><p>Draw the drone as a system of parts and say which part talks to which.</p></li>\n<li><p>Describe what each sensor measures and what the drone does with it.</p></li>\n<li><p>Convert units across several steps without losing track of what a number means.</p></li>\n<li><p>Use dimensional analysis to catch a wrong formula before you run any code.</p></li>\n<li><p>Read, write, and trace Python with lists, dictionaries, loops, and classes.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>A note on numbers: any thrust, mass, or voltage in this chapter that is not on your parts list is an example for practice, and it is marked as such. Real values come from datasheets and your own measurements, and we will collect them in later chapters.</p>\n", "items": [], "kind": "cp"}, {"id": "s2", "title": "1.1 How a quadcopter flies", "html": "<p>A quadcopter has four motors, and each motor spins a propeller that pushes air downward. Pushing air down pushes the drone up. The push is called <strong>thrust</strong>, and it is a force, so it is measured in newtons (N).</p>\n<p>First, three words you will use constantly. A <strong>force</strong> is a push or a pull, and forces are measured in <strong>newtons</strong> (N). One newton is about the weight of a small apple. <strong>Weight</strong> is the force with which Earth pulls on a mass: weight = mass × g, where g = 9.81 m/s² is the acceleration of gravity. The unit m/s² means \"meters per second, every second\": a freely falling object gains 9.81 m/s of speed each second. So a 1 kg mass weighs 9.81 N.</p>\n<p>To hover, the total thrust must equal the drone's weight. Weight is mass times the acceleration of gravity, W = m × g, with g = 9.81 m/s². If the four motors share the load equally, each one supplies a quarter of the weight:</p>\n<div class=\"math\">\n<span class=\"math display\">$$4T = mg \\;\\Rightarrow\\; T = \\dfrac{mg}{4}$$</span>\n</div>\n<p>Equation 1: hover condition, 4T = mg</p>\n<p><strong>Worked Example 1 (practice numbers).</strong> Suppose a drone has a mass of 0.9 kg. Its weight is 0.9 × 9.81 = 8.829 N, so each motor must supply 8.829 / 4 = 2.207 N to hover. Motor listings quote thrust in grams-force (gf): a thrust equal to the weight of that many grams, so 1 gf = 0.00981 N. To convert: 2.207 N ÷ 9.81 = 0.225 kg-force, which is 225 g.</p>\n<p>The <strong>thrust-to-weight ratio</strong> (TWR) is the most thrust the motors can make together divided by the drone's weight. A ratio of 1 means the drone can only just hover at full power, which leaves nothing for steering. Designers usually want a healthy margin above 2 so the drone can climb and correct itself. We will compute your actual ratio once you have measured thrust data.</p>\n<h3 id=\"steering-by-changing-motor-speeds\">Steering by changing motor speeds</h3>\n<p>The drone has no rudder or flaps. It steers only by running its four motors at different speeds. Name the two diagonal pairs of motors Pair A and Pair B. Pair A spins its propellers clockwise and Pair B spins them counterclockwise, so the twisting forces that each propeller puts on the frame cancel each other when all speeds are equal.</p>\n<table style=\"width:98%;\">\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 27%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 70%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">To do this</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Change the motor speeds like this</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Climb</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Speed up all four equally</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Descend</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Slow down all four equally</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Roll (tilt left or right)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Speed up the two motors on one side, slow the two on the other</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Pitch (tilt forward or back)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Speed up the two at the front, slow the two at the back (or the reverse)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Yaw (turn on the spot)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Speed up one pair and slow the other, so the twisting forces no longer cancel</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Tilting the drone tilts the thrust, which pushes the drone sideways. That is how it travels. The computer that decides these speed changes many times a second is the flight controller, which we meet in section 1.2.</p>\n", "items": ["cp0", "cp1"], "kind": "cp"}, {"id": "s3", "title": "1.2 Your drone as a system", "html": "<h3 id=\"words-you-will-see-in-the-diagram\">Words you will see in the diagram</h3>\n<p>Before the picture, here are plain-language meanings of the parts and terms it uses. Sections 1.3 and 1.4 explain the most important ones in depth. This table gives you just enough to read the diagram.</p>\n<table style=\"width:99%;\">\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 19%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 79%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Term</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">What it means</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Flight controller</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A small computer that reads the sensors and decides, many times each second, how fast each motor should spin.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">ESC (electronic speed controller)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The circuit board that switches electrical power to each motor to set its speed. Section 1.3 explains it fully.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">IMU</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A chip that measures how the drone is rotating and accelerating. Section 1.4 explains it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">GPS</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A receiver that works out position and speed from satellite signals.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Control link</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The radio that carries your stick commands to the drone.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Telemetry radio</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A second radio that sends data about the drone to the ground, and can receive commands back.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Raspberry Pi 5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A small but powerful general-purpose computer. Here it is the \"companion computer\" that handles the camera, data logging, and other heavy jobs.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Battery cell and S rating</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A drone battery is built from cells joined in a row. One cell supplies about 3.7 V in normal use, so a 6S battery (6 cells) supplies about 22.2 V.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Volt (V)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The unit for how hard a battery pushes electricity. Higher voltage means a harder push. Part III explains it properly.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Voltage regulator</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A small circuit that lowers the battery's voltage to the smaller voltage a computer needs.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"drone system diagram · 9 parts, power and signal links\">\n<figcaption>\ndrone system diagram · 9 parts, power and signal links\n</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p>drone system diagram · 9 parts, power and signal links</p>\n<p>Read the drawing from left to right: sensors and your control link (the radio that carries your stick commands) feed the flight controller, which sends motor commands to the ESC, which delivers the battery's power to the motors. The Raspberry Pi 5 sits beside the flight controller as a second computer. It is far more powerful, but it is not built for strict timing, so it handles the camera feed, the telemetry radio, and other heavy jobs instead of the control loop. The heavier colored lines carry power and the thin grey lines carry signals. The Pi and the flight controller get their power through a voltage regulator, because a battery's voltage is too high for them. Anything marked open is a decision you will make with more knowledge in later chapters.</p>\n", "items": ["cp2"], "kind": "cp"}, {"id": "s4", "title": "Angles first: degrees and radians", "html": "<p>Before we use rotation speeds in the parts list, you need one idea: how to measure an angle. You will see \"rad/s\" in the next section, and this is where it comes from. An angle measures an amount of turning. You already know one unit for it, the <strong>degree</strong>: a full circle is 360°. That number is a historical choice, picked because 360 divides evenly in many ways. It has no deeper connection to circles.</p>\n<p>The <strong>radian</strong> is defined by the circle itself. Take a circle of radius r. Walk along its edge a distance equal to exactly one radius, then draw lines from the center to where you started and where you stopped. The angle between those lines is <strong>1 radian</strong>. Since the whole edge of a circle is 2πr long, a full circle holds 2π radians, which is about 6.283.</p>\n<p>That gives the key facts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>360° = 2π rad, so <strong>180° = π rad</strong></p></li>\n<li><p>1 rad ≈ 57.3°</p></li>\n<li><p>to convert degrees to radians, multiply by π/180; to convert radians to degrees, multiply by 180/π</p></li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"width:97%;\">\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 20%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 36%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 41%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Degrees</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Radians (exact)</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Radians (decimal)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">30°</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">π/6</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">0.524</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">45°</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">π/4</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">0.785</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">90°</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">π/2</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1.571</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">180°</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">π</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3.142</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">360°</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2π</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">6.283</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Why engineers prefer radians.</strong> With radians, the distance traveled along a circle's edge is simply arc length = radius × angle, with the angle in radians. With degrees you would need an extra conversion constant every time. When we compute propeller tip speed in section 1.5, we will use exactly this: speed = (angular speed in rad/s) × (radius). Almost every formula in rotation, motors, and control assumes radians.</p>\n<p>A radian is a ratio of two lengths, an arc length divided by a radius, so the meters cancel. That is why it behaves like a number with no true unit, and why it was allowed to disappear in the dimensional analysis in Assignment 1. We still write \"rad\" to remind readers which angle unit we mean.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked Example A.</strong> A gyroscope reads a rotation rate of 90 degrees per second. In radians per second: 90 × π / 180 = 1.571 rad/s. A motor turning at 3000 rpm is 3000 revolutions × 2π rad per revolution ÷ 60 s = 314 rad/s.</p>\n<p>Calculators have the same trap: they have a degree mode and a radian mode, and being in the wrong one gives plausible but wrong answers. When a drone's tilt looks 57 times too large or too small in your code, suspect this mix-up first.</p>\n", "items": ["cp3", "cp4", "cp5"], "kind": "cp"}, {"id": "s5", "title": "1.3 Your parts list", "html": "<p>Your motors are firm; most other parts are still candidates. This table records each part, its job, and how settled the choice is, using only what you have told me. Rows marked \"open\" are decisions still ahead of you, and later chapters give you what you need to make them.</p>\n<table style=\"width:99%;\">\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 11%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 30%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 53%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 4%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Part</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Its job</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Your pick</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Status</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Motors (4)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Spin the propellers to make thrust</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">GEPRC EM3115 900KV</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Concrete</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Propellers (4)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Push air downward</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Not chosen yet</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Open</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">ESC (electronic speed controller)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Turns commands into the high current the motors need</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SEQURE Blueson A2 4-in-1, 70A, 6S/8S</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Considering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Flight controller</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Reads sensors, runs the control loop, tells the ESC how fast to spin each motor</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Not chosen; a Raspberry Pi Pico is one candidate</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Open</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Companion computer</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Heavier jobs: camera processing, logging, high-level decisions</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Raspberry Pi 5 (8 GB), which you own</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Likely</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Microcontrollers</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Small, fast, predictable chips</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2x Raspberry Pi Pico</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Likely</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">IMU (gyro + accelerometer)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Measures rotation and acceleration</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">TDK ICM-42688-P</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Considering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">GPS</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Measures position and speed from satellites</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">HGLRC M100-5883</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Considering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Camera</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Images for vision and video</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Raspberry Pi Camera Module 3 Wide</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Considering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Battery</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Stores the energy</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Not chosen</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Open</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Voltage regulator</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Steps the battery voltage down for the Pi, flight controller, and sensors</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Not chosen</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Open</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Radio link</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Carries your stick commands (the control link, which normally connects to the flight controller)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Your own controller; receiver not chosen</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Open</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Telemetry radio</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Sends flight data to the ground and receives ground commands, using the Pi in your plan</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Not chosen</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Open</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Frame hardware</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Holds everything together</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Standoffs (you have them), M3 heat-set inserts; 3D-printed frame, PLA-LW for the prototype and ASA Aero for the final, with parts hollowed out for components such as the Pi</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Considering</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3 id=\"what-is-an-esc\">What is an ESC?</h3>\n<p>A propeller motor in a drone is a <strong>brushless</strong> motor. It has no brushes inside, and that is what makes it light, efficient, and long-lasting. The price is that it cannot simply be connected to a battery and spun. It has three wires leading to coils, and the coils must be switched on in the right order, at exactly the right moments, thousands of times per second, or the motor just twitches. A small circuit board does that switching. It is the <strong>ESC</strong>, the electronic speed controller.</p>\n<p>The ESC sits between three things:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>The flight controller</strong> tells it how fast the motor should turn, as a number sent over a wire many times a second. Common ways of sending that number have names such as DShot, which you will meet in the radio and sensors part of the book.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>The battery</strong> supplies the power, which is high current and high voltage.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>The motor</strong> receives the three switched power wires.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Inside the ESC are a small microcontroller running <strong>firmware</strong> (the listings you found mention BLHeli_32 and AM32, which are firmware families), and a set of power transistors called MOSFETs that act as very fast electronic switches. The microcontroller decides when to turn each switch on. The more power it lets through, the faster the motor spins.</p>\n<p>A useful way to remember the split: <strong>the flight controller decides, and the ESC obeys.</strong> The flight controller works out how fast each motor should turn to keep the drone level. The ESC is the muscle that actually delivers the electrical power.</p>\n<p>Two terms in your parts listing now make sense:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>4-in-1</strong> means four separate ESC circuits, one for each motor, built onto a single board. That saves weight and wiring compared with four separate ESCs.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>70A and 6S/8S</strong> are limits. 70A is the current each of the four circuits is rated to carry, and 6S/8S means it is designed for batteries of 6 to 8 cells. Always read the datasheet for the difference between continuous and brief peak current ratings, because exceeding either can burn the board.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>ESCs are also a safety topic. A motor drawing a heavy current through a thin or loose wire gets hot, and an ESC that overheats can fail while the drone is in the air. We will return to this when we choose the battery and design the wiring.</p>\n<h3 id=\"decoding-the-motor-name\">Decoding the motor name</h3>\n<p>By the usual naming convention, \"3115\" means a stator 31 mm wide and 15 mm tall. Check this on the datasheet, because the datasheet is the authority and naming conventions have exceptions. \"900KV\" means the motor, with no propeller on it, turns about 900 revolutions per minute (rpm) for each volt applied.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked Example 2.</strong> On a 6S battery, the nominal voltage is 6 × 3.7 V = 22.2 V. The unloaded speed is about 900 × 22.2 = 19,980 rpm. In radians per second, that is 19,980 × 2π / 60 ≈ 2,092 rad/s. With a propeller attached the motor turns slower, so treat this as a ceiling, not a prediction.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-a-raspberry-pi-5-is-not-usually-the-flight-controller\">Why a Raspberry Pi 5 is not usually the flight controller</h3>\n<p>A flight controller has to read sensors and update motor commands on a fixed schedule, often hundreds or thousands of times per second, and a late update can make the drone wobble. A small microcontroller can keep a strict schedule. A Raspberry Pi 5 runs a full operating system that can pause a program briefly to do other work, which is why it more commonly handles heavy jobs that can tolerate small delays. This is the usual design split, and you will test it yourself when we study timing in later chapters.</p>\n", "items": ["cp6", "cp7", "cp8", "cp9"], "kind": "cp"}, {"id": "s6", "title": "1.4 Sensors: how the drone knows what is happening", "html": "<p>A drone cannot see its own tilt the way you can. Every decision it makes starts with a sensor turning something physical into a number. Here are the sensors that matter for your build.</p>\n<table style=\"width:99%;\">\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 8%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 43%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 11%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 35%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Sensor</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">What it measures</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Unit</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">What the drone does with it</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Gyroscope</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">How fast the drone is rotating</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">degrees or radians per second</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Fast stabilization: it notices a tilt starting and corrects it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Accelerometer</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Acceleration, including the constant pull of gravity</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">m/s²</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Finds which way is down when the drone is not accelerating much</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">IMU</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A gyroscope and accelerometer in one chip; your ICM-42688-P is a 6-axis IMU (3 gyroscope axes, 3 accelerometer axes)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">both of the above</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The core of flight stabilization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">GPS (a GNSS receiver)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Position and speed, from satellite signals</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">degrees, m, m/s</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Outdoor position hold and navigation; updates much more slowly than an IMU</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Barometer</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Air pressure, which changes with height</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">pascals</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Estimating altitude (not on your list yet)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Magnetometer</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The direction of Earth's magnetic field</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">tesla</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Compass heading (not on your list yet; check the GPS datasheet to see whether it includes one)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Camera</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Light, as an image</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">pixels</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Vision tasks and video</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3 id=\"no-single-sensor-is-enough\">No single sensor is enough</h3>\n<p>Each sensor has a weakness. The gyroscope is fast and smooth, but small errors add up, so its answer slowly drifts. The accelerometer does not drift, but it is noisy, and motor vibration shakes it. GPS does not drift either, but it only updates a few times per second and does not work well indoors. The solution is <strong>sensor fusion</strong>: a program that blends all of them, trusting each for what it does best. You will write a simple version in the sensors part of this book.</p>\n<p>Sensors also need a way to talk to the computer. The three common wiring styles are called SPI, I2C, and UART, and Part V explains each from scratch.</p>\n", "items": ["cp10", "cp11"], "kind": "cp"}, {"id": "s7", "title": "Unit Quiz 1: The drone and its parts", "html": "<p>Eight questions on sections 1.1 to 1.4 and the angles section. They are harder than the checkpoints: most connect two or more ideas, and one asks you to reason in words. Take your time, and use the contents list to look things up.</p><table><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Questions</th></tr><tr><td>Force, newton, weight, g</td><td>1, 2</td></tr><tr><td>Hover condition and thrust-to-weight ratio</td><td>1, 8</td></tr><tr><td>Steering with motor speeds</td><td>2, 8</td></tr><tr><td>System of parts (flight controller, IMU, GPS, radios, Pi, regulator)</td><td>3, 4, 7</td></tr><tr><td>Degrees and radians</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>Motors, KV, battery cells and voltage</td><td>5, 7, 8</td></tr><tr><td>ESC</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Raspberry Pi versus flight controller (timing)</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>Sensors and sensor fusion</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>SI units, prefixes, conversion</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>Precision, accuracy, uncertainty</td><td>6</td></tr></table>", "items": ["q1_1", "q1_2", "q1_3", "q1_4", "q1_5", "q1_6", "q1_7", "q1_8"], "kind": "quiz"}, {"id": "s8", "title": "1.5 Measuring things: units, prefixes, and conversion", "html": "<p>In 1999, NASA lost the Mars Climate Orbiter because one team's software produced a result in pound-force seconds while another team's software expected newton seconds. Both teams did their arithmetic correctly. They simply did not agree on units. Your drone has the same traps: motors quoted in grams, batteries in milliamp-hours, propellers in inches, and physics formulas that want newtons, coulombs, and meters.</p>\n<p><strong>Rule 1:</strong> Every physical number has a unit. Write the unit every time, including in your work on paper.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-si-system\">The SI system</h3>\n<p>Scientists and engineers use the International System of Units, called SI. It has four base units you need now, and every other unit is built from them.</p>\n<table style=\"width:97%;\">\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 42%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 27%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 27%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Quantity</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Unit</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Symbol</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Length</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">meter</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">m</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Mass</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">kilogram</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">kg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Time</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">second</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Electric current</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">ampere</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table style=\"width:97%;\">\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 31%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 32%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 32%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Derived quantity</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Unit</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Built from</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Speed</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">meter per second</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">m/s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Acceleration</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">m/s²</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">m/s per second</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Force</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">newton (N)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">kg·m/s²</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Energy</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">joule (J)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">N·m</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Power</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">watt (W)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">J/s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Charge</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">coulomb (C)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A·s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Voltage</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">volt (V)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">J/C</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3 id=\"metric-prefixes\">Metric prefixes</h3>\n<table style=\"width:97%;\">\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 29%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 41%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Prefix</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Symbol</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">mega</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">M</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1,000,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">kilo</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">k</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">centi</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">c</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1/100</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">milli</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">m</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1/1,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">micro</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">µ</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1/1,000,000</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Capital M means mega and lowercase m means milli. They differ by a factor of a billion, which is exactly the kind of mistake a spreadsheet will not catch for you.</p>\n<h3 id=\"converting-by-multiplying-by-1\">Converting by multiplying by 1</h3>\n<p>Since 1 km = 1000 m, the fraction (1000 m) / (1 km) equals exactly 1. Multiplying by 1 never changes a value, but it changes the unit. Arrange the fractions so the unwanted units cancel.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked Example 3.</strong> Convert 90 km/h to m/s.</p>\n<p>90 km/h × (1000 m / 1 km) × (1 h / 3600 s) = 90 × 1000 / 3600 = 25 m/s</p>\n<p><strong>Worked Example 4.</strong> A battery is 1500 mAh at a nominal 22.2 V (a 6S pack). How much energy is stored, in watt-hours and in joules?</p>\n<p>Charge first: 1500 mAh = 1.5 Ah, and 1 Ah = 3600 C, so the charge is 1.5 × 3600 = 5,400 C.</p>\n<p>Energy is voltage times charge, and volt × coulomb = joule. So E = 22.2 V × 5,400 C = 119,880 J.</p>\n<p>In watt-hours, E = 22.2 V × 1.5 Ah = 33.3 Wh. The two answers agree, because 33.3 Wh × 3600 s/h = 119,880 J.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked Example 5 (practice numbers).</strong> A 7-inch propeller spins at 19,980 rpm. How fast is the tip moving, in m/s?</p>\n<p>Step 1, radius in meters: 7 in is the diameter, so the radius is 3.5 in = 3.5 × 0.0254 = 0.0889 m.</p>\n<p>Step 2, rotation rate in rad/s: one revolution is 2π radians and one minute is 60 s, so 19,980 × 2π / 60 ≈ 2,092 rad/s.</p>\n<p>Step 3, tip speed is angular speed times radius: 2,092 × 0.0889 ≈ 186 m/s.</p>\n<p>That is more than half the speed of sound, which tells you real propellers never spin that fast unloaded in practice, and why tip speed is something designers watch.</p>\n", "items": ["cp12", "cp13", "cp14"], "kind": "cp"}, {"id": "s9", "title": "1.6 Scientific notation, dimensional analysis, and precision", "html": "<h3 id=\"scientific-notation\">Scientific notation</h3>\n<p>Very large and very small numbers are written as a number between 1 and 10 times a power of ten: 19,980 = 1.998 × 10⁴ and 0.00042 = 4.2 × 10⁻⁴. To multiply, multiply the front numbers and add the exponents. To divide, divide the front numbers and subtract the exponents. In Python you type 1.998e4 and 4.2e-4.</p>\n<h3 id=\"dimensional-analysis\">Dimensional analysis</h3>\n<p>A correct formula has the same kind of unit on both sides of the equals sign. Checking that is called <strong>dimensional analysis</strong>, and it catches errors before you ever run code.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked Example 6.</strong> Is kinetic energy ½ m v² really an energy? Its unit is kg × (m/s)² = kg·m²/s². One newton is kg·m/s², and one joule is a newton times a meter, so a joule is kg·m²/s². The units match, so the formula passes.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked Example 7.</strong> Later you will meet the drag force on a moving object, F = ½ × ρ × v² × C × A, where ρ (the Greek letter rho) is the air density, meaning the mass of air in each cubic meter, about 1.2 kg/m³ near sea level, v is speed, A is area in m², and C is a number with no unit. Check it: kg/m³ × (m/s)² × m² = kg·m²·m² / (m³·s²) = kg·m/s², which is a newton. The formula passes, and you now trust it before you have used it once.</p>\n<p>A failed check is just as useful. If a formula for distance gives m·s instead of m, something is wrong in the formula or in your memory of it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"significant-figures-how-many-digits-can-you-trust\">Significant figures: how many digits can you trust?</h3>\n<p>A measurement is never perfectly exact. A kitchen scale that reads 1200 g does not tell you the mass is 1200.0000 g, only that it is close to 1200 g, within the scale's limits. The digits in a number that carry real information are its <strong>significant figures</strong> (often shortened to \"sig figs\"). Writing more digits than you actually know is a quiet way of lying, and it is one of the most common mistakes in student engineering.</p>\n<p><strong>Which digits count.</strong> Five rules decide it:</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Nonzero digits always count.</p></li>\n<li><p>Zeros between nonzero digits count.</p></li>\n<li><p>Zeros at the start of a number never count. They only show where the decimal point sits.</p></li>\n<li><p>Zeros at the end count only if the number has a decimal point.</p></li>\n<li><p>Exact numbers have unlimited significant figures. These are counted things (4 motors) and definitions (1000 m in a km, 3600 s in an hour, 1 in = 0.0254 m exactly).</p></li>\n</ol>\n<table style=\"width:98%;\">\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 15%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 21%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 61%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Number</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Sig figs</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">4.82</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">All nonzero digits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1007</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The zeros sit between nonzero digits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">0.0042</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The leading zeros only place the decimal point</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">0.0120</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The trailing zero after the decimal point counts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1.200 × 10³</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Scientific notation shows exactly which digits count</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1200</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">unclear (2 to 4)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Trailing zeros with no decimal point are ambiguous</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">4 motors</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">unlimited</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A counted number is exact</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The row for 1200 is why scientific notation is so useful. Writing 1.2 × 10³ says you trust two digits, and 1.200 × 10³ says you trust four. Nothing is left to guess.</p>\n<p><strong>Calculating with them.</strong> Two rules cover nearly everything:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Multiplying or dividing:</strong> the answer has as many significant figures as the input that has the fewest.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Adding or subtracting:</strong> the answer keeps as many decimal places as the input that has the fewest decimal places.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>And one habit protects you from rounding mistakes: keep the extra digits through the middle of a calculation, and round only the final answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked Example 8.</strong> A scale reads your test drone's mass as 0.912 kg, which is 3 significant figures. Find the hover thrust per motor. The 4 motors and the 2 in the formula are exact, and g = 9.81 has 3 significant figures:</p>\n<p>0.912 × 9.81 ÷ 4 = 2.23668 N</p>\n<p>The fewest significant figures in the inputs is 3, so the answer is <strong>2.24 N</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked Example 9.</strong> You add three part masses: 143.2 g (frame, measured to a tenth of a gram), 45 g (a part weighed to the nearest gram), and 187.5 g.</p>\n<p>143.2 + 45 + 187.5 = 375.7 g</p>\n<p>The 45 g has no decimal places, so the sum can have none either: <strong>376 g</strong>. A single coarse measurement limits the whole total.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked Example 10.</strong> Convert 90 km/h to m/s. The 90 has 2 significant figures, and 1000 m/km and 3600 s/h are exact, so 90 × 1000 / 3600 = 25 m/s is reported with 2 significant figures, which is exactly what \"25\" shows. If the speed were 90.0 km/h, you would write 25.0 m/s.</p>\n<p>Python can round to a number of significant figures for you. You will see how in section 1.7, after you have met Python.</p>\n<h3 id=\"precision-accuracy-and-uncertainty\">Precision, accuracy, and uncertainty</h3>\n<p>Two words that sound alike mean different things. <strong>Precision</strong> is how close repeated measurements are to each other. <strong>Accuracy</strong> is how close they are to the true value. A thrust stand that reads 11.8, 11.7, and 11.8 N on three runs is precise. If the load cell is miscalibrated by 5%, it is precise but not accurate, and taking more runs will never fix that. Averaging reduces random scatter; it cannot remove a consistent error.</p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"precision vs accuracy · four target patterns\">\n<figcaption>\nprecision vs accuracy · four target patterns\n</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p>precision vs accuracy · four target patterns</p>\n<p>A thrust stand with a miscalibrated load cell looks like the second target: every run agrees, and every run is wrong in the same direction.</p>\n<p>When you can, write a measurement with its <strong>uncertainty</strong>, the amount you could be off by. A reading of 11.8 ± 0.1 N says the true value probably lies between 11.7 and 11.9 N. Dividing the uncertainty by the value gives a <strong>percent uncertainty</strong>, which makes different measurements comparable.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked Example 11.</strong> A thrust test reads 1200 g with an uncertainty of ±10 g. The percent uncertainty is 10 / 1200 = 0.83%. In newtons, the thrust is 1.2 kg × 9.81 = 11.772 N, and 0.83% of that is about 0.10 N, so you report <strong>11.8 ± 0.1 N</strong>. For a product or quotient of measurements, a safe rule of thumb is that the percent uncertainties add up. This is why one sloppy measurement can spoil a result built from several good ones.</p>\n<h3 id=\"percent-error\">Percent error</h3>\n<p>When you compare a prediction with a real measurement, the size of the miss is the <strong>percent error</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"math\">\n<span class=\"math display\">$$\\text{percent error} = \\dfrac{|\\text{measured} - \\text{predicted}|}{\\text{predicted}} \\times 100\\%$$</span>\n</div>\n<p>Equation 2: percent error</p>\n<p>Engineering is largely the work of explaining where that error comes from: your measurement uncertainty, a simplification in your model, or a mistake. A simulation that is off by 5% teaches you something. A simulation you never compared to reality teaches you nothing. Significant figures tell you when a gap is just noise: if your prediction and your measurement agree to within their uncertainties, the gap is not meaningful.</p>\n", "items": ["cp15", "cp16", "cp17", "cp18", "cp19", "cp20", "cp21", "cp22"], "kind": "cp"}, {"id": "s10", "title": "Unit Quiz 2: Measurement, angles, and uncertainty", "html": "<p>Eight questions on sections 1.5 and 1.6, plus the angles section. Several chain two or three steps together.</p><table><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Questions</th></tr><tr><td>Force, newton, weight, g</td><td>5, 8</td></tr><tr><td>Hover condition and thrust-to-weight ratio</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>Degrees and radians</td><td>3, 4</td></tr><tr><td>SI units, prefixes, conversion</td><td>1, 3</td></tr><tr><td>Energy and charge (J, Wh, C)</td><td>1, 2</td></tr><tr><td>Propeller tip speed</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Dimensional analysis</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Significant figures</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>Precision, accuracy, uncertainty</td><td>6, 7</td></tr><tr><td>Percent error</td><td>8</td></tr></table>", "items": ["q2_1", "q2_2", "q2_3", "q2_4", "q2_5", "q2_6", "q2_7", "q2_8"], "kind": "quiz"}, {"id": "s11", "title": "1.7 Python refresher: from variables to classes", "html": "<p>You have used Python to run a robot, so none of this is new territory, but you told me lists and dictionaries never quite clicked, and that classes and async need a second look. We rebuild them here with drone examples. Type every example yourself instead of pasting it, because your fingers remember what your eyes skim past. All numbers in the code are practice numbers.</p>\n<h3 id=\"variables-carry-units-in-their-names\">Variables carry units in their names</h3>\n<p>mass_kg = 0.9<br />\nspeed_kmh = 90<br />\nspeed_ms = speed_kmh * 1000 / 3600<br />\nprint(speed_ms) # 25.0</p>\n<p>Putting the unit in the name (mass_kg, speed_ms) is a small habit with a big payoff. When you read mass_kg * g, you can check the units without looking anything up.</p>\n<h3 id=\"functions-and-default-values\">Functions and default values</h3>\n<p>G = 9.81 # m/s^2, a constant: written in capitals by convention<br />\n<br />\ndef hover_thrust_per_motor(mass_kg, motors=4):<br />\nreturn mass_kg * G / motors<br />\n<br />\nprint(hover_thrust_per_motor(0.9)) # uses motors=4<br />\nprint(hover_thrust_per_motor(1.5, 6)) # a six-motor drone</p>\n<p>A parameter with an equals sign has a <strong>default value</strong>: it is used when the caller leaves that argument out. You wrote functions like this in your robot library, where speed=525 is what you get unless you say otherwise. Defaults make common calls short and unusual calls possible.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lists-an-ordered-row-of-values\">Lists: an ordered row of values</h3>\n<p>A list holds values in order. Each value has a position number called an <strong>index</strong>, and counting starts at 0, not 1.</p>\n<p>thrust_g = [1850, 1820, 1840, 1790] # practice numbers, one per motor<br />\nprint(thrust_g[0]) # 1850, the first item<br />\nprint(thrust_g[-1]) # 1790, the last item<br />\nprint(thrust_g[1:3]) # [1820, 1840]: from index 1 up to, not including, 3<br />\nthrust_g.append(1805) # add to the end<br />\nprint(len(thrust_g)) # 5<br />\n<br />\ntotal = 0<br />\nfor t in thrust_g: # visit each item in order<br />\ntotal += t<br />\nprint(total / len(thrust_g)) # the average</p>\n<p>A good mental picture: a list is a row of numbered mailboxes starting at box 0. Use a list when the order matters or when you have many of the same kind of thing, like sensor readings over time.</p>\n<h3 id=\"dictionaries-look-things-up-by-name\">Dictionaries: look things up by name</h3>\n<p>A dictionary stores pairs, each a <strong>key</strong> and a <strong>value</strong>. You find a value by its key, not by a position.</p>\n<p>part_mass_g = {\"motor\": 52, \"esc\": 38, \"camera\": 4} # practice numbers<br />\nprint(part_mass_g[\"esc\"]) # 38<br />\npart_mass_g[\"gps\"] = 6 # add a new pair<br />\nprint(\"gps\" in part_mass_g) # True<br />\nprint(part_mass_g.get(\"radar\", 0)) # 0: a safe lookup with a fallback<br />\n<br />\nfor name, mass in part_mass_g.items(): # visit every pair<br />\nprint(name, mass)</p>\n<p>Use a dictionary when each value has a meaningful name, like a parts list. Looking up a key that is not there raises an error (KeyError), which is why .get() with a fallback is handy.</p>\n<p>Lists and dictionaries combine. A list of dictionaries is a table:</p>\n<p>parts = [<br />\n{\"name\": \"motor\", \"mass_g\": 52, \"count\": 4},<br />\n{\"name\": \"esc\", \"mass_g\": 38, \"count\": 1},<br />\n]<br />\ntotal_g = 0<br />\nfor p in parts:<br />\ntotal_g += p[\"mass_g\"] * p[\"count\"]<br />\nprint(total_g) # 246</p>\n<h3 id=\"classes-your-own-kind-of-object\">Classes: your own kind of object</h3>\n<p>When several pieces of data belong together with the functions that use them, a <strong>class</strong> bundles them. Your Robot class did this for axle length, wheel diameter, and motor ports. Here is the same idea for a drone:</p>\n<p>class Drone:<br />\ndef __init__(self, name, mass_kg, motor_count=4):<br />\nself.name = name<br />\nself.mass_kg = mass_kg<br />\nself.motor_count = motor_count<br />\n<br />\ndef weight_n(self):<br />\nreturn self.mass_kg * 9.81<br />\n<br />\ndef hover_thrust_per_motor_n(self):<br />\nreturn self.weight_n() / self.motor_count<br />\n<br />\nquad = Drone(\"test quad\", 0.9)<br />\nbig = Drone(\"big hex\", 1.5, 6)<br />\nprint(quad.hover_thrust_per_motor_n()) # about 2.207<br />\nprint(big.hover_thrust_per_motor_n()) # 2.4525</p>\n<p>Drone(...) makes a new <strong>object</strong> and runs __init__ to fill it in. self means \"the object this method is working on\". When you call quad.weight_n(), Python quietly passes quad in as self, so self.mass_kg is quad's mass. When you call big.weight_n(), self is big. Two objects, one set of instructions, and no mixing between them.</p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"objects and classes · two Drone objects, one shared class\">\n<figcaption>\nobjects and classes · two Drone objects, one shared class\n</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p>objects and classes · two Drone objects, one shared class</p>\n<p>The class is written once. Each object keeps its own data, and the arrows show which object you are working on whenever a method runs.</p>\n<h3 id=\"async-and-await\">async and await</h3>\n<p>Some code has to wait: for a motor to finish turning, for a sensor to answer, for a radio message to arrive. An async def function can pause at an await line and let other work happen during the wait.</p>\n<p>import asyncio<br />\n<br />\nasync def spin_up(name, seconds):<br />\nprint(name, \"starting\")<br />\nawait asyncio.sleep(seconds) # pauses here, without freezing everything<br />\nprint(name, \"done\")<br />\n<br />\nasync def main():<br />\nawait spin_up(\"motor 1\", 1) # finishes first (1 s)<br />\nawait asyncio.gather(spin_up(\"motor 2\", 1),<br />\nspin_up(\"motor 3\", 1)) # run together (about 1 s total)<br />\n<br />\nasyncio.run(main())</p>\n<p>The first line takes one second, then the pair takes another second, not two, because they wait at the same time. Your robot programs use the same pattern with runloop.run(main()) in place of asyncio.run(main()). In those programs, some motor lines have await in front and some do not. We will study exactly what that changes in the review chapter on the habits in your code.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reading-errors-and-testing\">Reading errors and testing</h3>\n<p>When Python stops with an error, read the last line first: it names the problem. The lines above it show where. A habit that will carry through the whole book is to test small functions with assert, which does nothing when its condition is true and stops with an error when it is false:</p>\n<p>assert abs(hover_thrust_per_motor(0.9) - 2.20725) &lt; 1e-6</p>\n<p>The abs(...) &lt; 1e-6 pattern compares decimals safely, because computers store them with tiny rounding errors. The autograder you will use for the assignment is built on this idea.</p>\n<h3 id=\"python-and-the-ideas-from-earlier-sections\">Python and the ideas from earlier sections</h3>\n<p>Now that you know the basics of Python, you can use it for two ideas from earlier in the chapter.</p>\n<p><strong>Significant figures in Python.</strong> Python does not track significant figures for you, and it prints every digit it computes. Look at what happens with Worked Example 8:</p>\n<p>x = 0.912 * 9.81 / 4<br />\nprint(x) # 2.2366800000000002 &lt;- the tail is rounding noise<br />\nprint(round(x, 2)) # 2.24 &lt;- 2 decimal places<br />\nprint(f\"{x:.3g}\") # 2.24 &lt;- 3 significant figures</p>\n<p>The long tail appears because computers store decimals in binary, which cannot represent most of them exactly. Those extra digits are not information about the drone. Use round(x, n) for a number of decimal places and the format f\"{x:.3g}\" for a number of significant figures.</p>\n<p><strong>Angles in Python.</strong> In the section on angles, we said that rotation formulas assume radians. Python's trigonometry functions expect radians too. If you pass degrees, you get a wrong answer with no error message:</p>\n<p>import math<br />\nprint(math.radians(90)) # 1.5707963267948966<br />\nprint(math.degrees(math.pi)) # 180.0<br />\nprint(math.sin(90)) # 0.8939966636005579 (treats 90 as radians!)<br />\nprint(math.sin(math.radians(90))) # 1.0</p>\n<h3 id=\"good-programming-habits-from-day-one\">Good programming habits from day one</h3>\n<p>Code is read far more often than it is written, and the reader is usually you, a few weeks later. Habits that look like extra work on day one save hours later. Here are the habits we will practice from now on, each with its reason.</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>Name things for what they mean, with units.</strong> mass_kg tells you more than m. Wrong units are a leading cause of drone bugs, and a name with the unit in it exposes the mistake.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Give constants a name and write them once.</strong> A bare number such as 9.81 in the middle of a formula is called a <em>magic number</em>, because a reader cannot tell what it is. Write G = 9.81 once at the top. If you ever need to change it, you change one line, not twenty.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>One function, one job.</strong> A small function that does one thing is easy to test, reuse, and understand.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Write a one-line docstring.</strong> It states what the function does and its units, so you do not have to read the code to use it.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Test as you go.</strong> After you write a function, write an assert that checks a case you worked out by hand.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Comments explain why, not what.</strong> # g in m/s^2 is useful. # add one to x next to x = x + 1 is noise.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Do not repeat yourself.</strong> If you copy and paste a calculation, turn it into a function.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Here is the same calculation written badly and well:</p>\n<p># Hard to read and hard to trust<br />\ndef f(a, b):<br />\nreturn a * 9.81 / b<br />\n<br />\n# Clear, reusable, and tested<br />\nG = 9.81 # m/s^2, acceleration of gravity near Earth's surface<br />\n<br />\ndef hover_thrust_per_motor_n(mass_kg, motors=4):<br />\n\"\"\"Return the thrust in newtons each motor must supply to hover.\"\"\"<br />\nreturn mass_kg * G / motors<br />\n<br />\nassert abs(hover_thrust_per_motor_n(0.9) - 2.20725) &lt; 1e-6</p>\n<p>You may notice that you already follow some of these habits in your robot code. Later in the book, a review chapter will go through that code and show the reason behind each good habit it already contains.</p>\n", "items": ["cp23", "cp24", "cp25", "cp26", "cp27", "cp28", "cp29"], "kind": "cp"}, {"id": "s12", "title": "Unit Quiz 3: Python and good habits", "html": "<p>Eight questions on section 1.7. Read the code carefully, work it out by hand, and then check.</p><table><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Questions</th></tr><tr><td>Significant figures</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>Variables, functions, defaults</td><td>2, 5</td></tr><tr><td>Lists</td><td>1, 7, 8</td></tr><tr><td>Dictionaries</td><td>1, 8</td></tr><tr><td>Classes and self</td><td>3, 8</td></tr><tr><td>async and await</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>Errors and assert</td><td>6, 7</td></tr><tr><td>Good programming habits</td><td>5, 8</td></tr></table>", "items": ["q3_1", "q3_2", "q3_3", "q3_4", "q3_5", "q3_6", "q3_7", "q3_8"], "kind": "quiz"}, {"id": "s13", "title": "How everything in this chapter connects", "html": "<p>Ideas in this chapter lean on each other. This map shows, for each topic, what you needed first and where it leads. Use it to review, and to see why the chapter is in the order it is.</p>\n<table style=\"width:99%;\">\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 21%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 24%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 53%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Topic</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">You needed first</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">It connects to</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Force, newton, weight, g</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Nothing (defined in 1.1)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Hover condition; thrust ratings in grams-force; energy and power later</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Hover condition, thrust-to-weight</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Weight, newtons</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Motor choice, battery size, flight time, control margin in later parts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Steering with motor speeds</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Thrust, four-motor layout</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The flight controller's mixing, PID control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">System of parts</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The glossary table</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Every later part chapter; the diagram is revisited in sensors, power, and build chapters</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Degrees and radians</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Circles from geometry</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">rad/s and rpm, gyroscope readings, tip speed, trigonometry in Python, control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Motors, KV, battery cells</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Volts and the S rating (glossary), rpm</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Unloaded speed, ESC limits, propeller choice, energy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">ESC</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Brushless motor, flight controller</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Current limits, wiring safety, firmware, DShot</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Raspberry Pi vs flight controller</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Timing, operating systems (idea only)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Telemetry, logging, vision, safety when the Pi stops</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Sensors and sensor fusion</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">IMU, GPS, rate and noise ideas</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Precision and accuracy, filtering, state estimation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SI units, prefixes, conversion</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Nothing</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Every calculation; names with units in Python</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Energy (J, Wh) and charge (C)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Volt, derived units</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Battery capacity, flight time, power draw</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Tip speed</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Radians, rpm, radius</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Propeller choice, noise, efficiency</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Scientific notation</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Powers of ten</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Prefixes, Python's 1e-6 notation, tolerances</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Dimensional analysis</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Derived units</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Checking formulas, spotting unit bugs in code</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Significant figures</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Measurement and rounding</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Uncertainty, percent error, Python rounding and formats</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Precision, accuracy, uncertainty</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Significant figures</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Sensor quality, thrust stand tests, simulation validation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Percent error</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Uncertainty</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Comparing a simulation to a flight test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Variables, functions, defaults</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Units and names</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Every program; test-driven habits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Lists and dictionaries</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Variables</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Sensor logs, parts lists, configuration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Classes and self</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Functions, dictionaries</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The Drone model, simulators, your robot's Robot class</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">async and await</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Functions</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Reading sensors without freezing, robot programs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Errors and assert</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Functions</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Testing everything you write, autograder tests</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Good programming habits</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">All the Python above</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Readable, testable code in every later chapter</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n", "items": [], "kind": "cp"}, {"id": "s14", "title": "Assignment 1", "html": "<p>The assignment has four tiers. Tiers 1 and 2 confirm you absorbed the chapter. Tier 3 asks you to combine ideas and debug, which is where real understanding shows. Tier 4 is optional and hard. Show units on every paper answer.</p>\n", "items": ["a1", "a2", "a3", "a4", "a5", "c_kmh_to_ms", "c_grams_force_to_newtons", "c_mah_to_coulombs", "c_battery_energy_wh", "c_unloaded_rpm", "c_hover_thrust_per_motor_n", "c_round_sig", "c_tip_speed_ms", "c_percent_error", "c_total_mass_g", "c_mass_by_name", "a16", "a17", "a18"], "kind": "asg"}, {"id": "s15", "title": "Chapter 1 Test", "html": "<p>Forty questions. Questions 1 to 26 cover every topic in the chapter, one by one. Questions 27 to 40 are connection questions: each needs several topics at once, and the last four ask you to explain your reasoning. The table shows where each topic is tested.</p><table><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Questions</th></tr><tr><td>Force, newton, weight, g</td><td>1, 28, 32, 34, 38</td></tr><tr><td>Hover condition and thrust-to-weight ratio</td><td>2, 28, 34, 39, 40</td></tr><tr><td>Steering with motor speeds</td><td>3, 37</td></tr><tr><td>System of parts (flight controller, IMU, GPS, radios, Pi, regulator)</td><td>4, 36, 37, 40</td></tr><tr><td>Degrees and radians</td><td>5, 14, 29, 30, 35</td></tr><tr><td>Motors, KV, battery cells and voltage</td><td>6, 7, 27, 36</td></tr><tr><td>ESC</td><td>8, 36, 37</td></tr><tr><td>Raspberry Pi versus flight controller (timing)</td><td>9, 37, 40</td></tr><tr><td>Sensors and sensor fusion</td><td>4, 10</td></tr><tr><td>SI units, prefixes, conversion</td><td>11, 12, 27, 28, 29, 32, 38</td></tr><tr><td>Energy and charge (J, Wh, C)</td><td>13, 27, 33</td></tr><tr><td>Propeller tip speed</td><td>14, 30</td></tr><tr><td>Scientific notation</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>Dimensional analysis</td><td>16, 33</td></tr><tr><td>Significant figures</td><td>17, 34</td></tr><tr><td>Precision, accuracy, uncertainty</td><td>18, 31, 38</td></tr><tr><td>Percent error</td><td>19, 31, 38</td></tr><tr><td>Variables, functions, defaults</td><td>20, 33, 35</td></tr><tr><td>Lists</td><td>21, 32, 39</td></tr><tr><td>Dictionaries</td><td>22, 32, 39</td></tr><tr><td>Classes and self</td><td>23, 39</td></tr><tr><td>async and await</td><td>24</td></tr><tr><td>Errors and assert</td><td>25</td></tr><tr><td>Good programming habits</td><td>26, 39</td></tr></table>", "items": ["t_1", "t_2", "t_3", "t_4", "t_5", "t_6", "t_7", "t_8", "t_9", "t_10", "t_11", "t_12", "t_13", "t_14", "t_15", "t_16", "t_17", "t_18", "t_19", "t_20", "t_21", "t_22", "t_23", "t_24", "t_25", "t_26", "t_27", "t_28", "t_29", "t_30", "t_31", "t_32", "t_33", "t_34", "t_35", "t_36", "t_37", "t_38", "t_39", "t_40"], "kind": "test"}]};
